    "verdict": "REVISE",
    "summary": "The revised plan resolves the prior API/configuration issues, but durable deduplication is not crash-safe, rate limiting is only advisory, and observation/link handling remains internally inconsistent.",
    "findings": [
      {
        "id": "REV-007",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — “Дедупликация алертов” / `check_route`",
        "evidence": "The plan sends a Telegram message and only then inserts the dedup record “after successful send.” These are separate durable systems and cannot be one SQLite transaction. If Telegram accepts the message, then the process/SQLite write fails before `mark_alerted` commits, the next process run sees no row and sends the same alert again—violating durable no-repeat acceptance criterion 2.",
        "fix": "Define an explicit delivery guarantee. For at-most-once alerts, durably create a pending/sent-suppression record before sending and accept possible lost messages on a crash; for at-least-once delivery, use a durable outbox and document duplicates as possible. Do not claim the external send and SQLite insert are transactional."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-008",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — Rate limiting / `fetch_cheapest` / `main()`",
        "evidence": "`MAX_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=60` produces only a startup warning when the configured polling schedule exceeds it; requests are not actually paced or rejected. For example, 30 routes at a 20-second interval make 90 requests/minute despite the configured 60-request budget. Retrying after 429 does not prevent the initial excess, and an in-route `Retry-After` sleep can also delay every later route in a sequential single-process loop.",
        "fix": "Enforce the configured budget with a shared monotonic rate limiter or fail configuration validation. Represent per-route retry deadlines without blocking unrelated routes, and schedule only due routes each cycle."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-009",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — acceptance criterion 2 versus “Фильтр актуальности”",
        "evidence": "Criterion 2 requires every observation to be written to `prices.db`, while the approach says an entry with `actual == false` is simply skipped. A valid API response marked non-actual is still an observation, but it will neither be persisted nor available for audit/debugging, contradicting the stated persistence behavior.",
        "fix": "Persist all fetched observations with an eligibility/actual flag, while excluding ineligible rows from the anomaly baseline and alert decision; update the schema and `get_recent_prices` filtering accordingly."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-010",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `build_purchase_link` and API-response fallback",
        "evidence": "The plan says the v3 `link` is a relative path requiring concatenation, but the file-level description says `build_purchase_link` “uses `entry[\"link\"]` if present,” without defining the required normalization. The official Data API description for this endpoint describes `link` as a code/path to append to an Aviasales search base. Passing it directly to Telegram yields a non-working relative link; concatenating without checking whether it is already absolute can also produce a malformed URL.",
        "fix": "Specify one canonical transformation: validate an absolute Aviasales URL, otherwise append the documented relative value to the documented Aviasales search base. Add deterministic tests for both relative and absolute/missing-link cases, including the alerted dates."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-011",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — acceptance criterion 1 / `check_route` sequence / history semantics",
        "evidence": "The required test describes the production sequence as “fetch → record → anomaly,” but the plan never states whether the just-fetched candidate is excluded from the median or whether `MIN_HISTORY_SAMPLES` means prior eligible observations. Implementations that query history after recording can satisfy the threshold with only four prior samples, and with a small configured history window can include the anomalous value in the baseline and suppress its own alert.",
        "fix": "Define the decision order explicitly: read the last `HISTORY_WINDOW` eligible prior observations, require at least `MIN_HISTORY_SAMPLES` prior rows, evaluate the candidate against that snapshot, then record it. Make the mocked end-to-end test assert this boundary condition."
      }
